<DocsLayout title="Infrastructure as code" description="The Terraform that declares the same scmle service on AWS and GCP, the policy check that needs no account, destroy without orphans, and what has not been applied." current="infrastructure">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Cloud and release</p>
  <h1>The same service,<br /><em>declared twice.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">Terraform (or OpenTofu) declares the same prediction service on AWS and on GCP. This page says what is declared, what is checked without an account, and what is not checked at all.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Nothing has been applied to an account.</strong> OpenTofu 1.13.5 passes <code>fmt -check</code> and <code>init -backend=false</code> plus <code>validate</code> on both modules and all six environments, the provider lock files are committed, and the continuous-integration job for infrastructure policy, format and validate passed on the audited commit; the result is recorded in the Phase 16 evidence. <code>plan</code> and <code>apply</code> have not been run, so no resource exists and no cost has been measured.</p></div>

  <h2>Layout</h2>
  <pre><code>{`deploy/terraform/
  modules/aws_environment/   one shared module per provider
  modules/gcp_environment/
  aws/environments/{dev,staging,production}/
  gcp/environments/{dev,staging,production}/`}</code></pre>
  <p>Each environment directory holds a <code>main.tf</code> that calls the shared module with its own sizing, budget and protection settings, a <code>variables.tf</code> for the values that only exist at deploy time (image digest, issuer, repository), and <code>outputs.tf</code>. The state location is not in the files: the S3 and GCS backends are empty blocks filled with <code>-backend-config</code> at <code>init</code>.</p>

  <h2>What is declared</h2>
  <DocTable label="Infrastructure declared on each provider" head={["Area", "AWS", "GCP"]} rows={matrix} />
  <p>Every taggable resource carries <code>scmle-environment=&lt;env&gt;</code>; the destroy-orphan checker finds leftovers by it.</p>
  <p><strong>Known gaps:</strong> the GCP module uses Google-managed encryption for Artifact Registry and Secret Manager (a customer key only on Cloud Storage); the AWS API Gateway default endpoint is used, with no custom domain or certificate; there is no WAF or Cloud Armor; there is no multi-region; Cloud Run containers cannot be made read-only through this resource; and the API Gateway access-log resource policy is not declared.</p>

  <h2>Checks that need no account</h2>
  <pre><code>{`python scripts/check_terraform_policy.py deploy/terraform   # always runs
scripts/terraform_checks.sh deploy/terraform                # fmt + validate if the tool exists`}</code></pre>
  <p>The policy checker parses the files and refuses:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a public ingress CIDR, a gateway route without an authorizer, a load balancer that is public without authenticating, tasks with public addresses, <code>allUsers</code> or <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code>, a disabled invoker check;</li>
    <li>storage, registries, log groups, secrets, topics or keys without encryption, buckets without a public-access block, mutable image tags, keys without rotation;</li>
    <li>wildcard IAM actions, wildcard principals (except in a Deny), wildcard resources (except the few calls that cannot be scoped; key policies are exempt), administrator or primitive roles, project-wide grants, federation trusts that do not match one subject exactly, missing deploy, train or serve identities, a <code>serve</code> role that can write, or a <code>deploy</code> role that can touch data, secrets or keys;</li>
    <li>a missing budget or notification, or a non-positive amount per environment;</li>
    <li>an image that is not a digest, or a digest variable that does not validate;</li>
    <li>credential-shaped text, literal secret attributes, secret-named defaults, secret values in an environment list, state, key and variable files, and a backend with settings in the file;</li>
    <li>missing resource limits, autoscaling or private networking, fewer than two alerts, and a production environment that runs fewer than two instances or can be destroyed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It reads text. It does not evaluate expressions, resolve modules or know the provider's schema, so it cannot tell that a file is valid Terraform and cannot see what a variable will hold. A pass means the files declare what the policy requires, nothing more. The reader supports the subset the repository uses (one attribute per line, no heredocs) and raises on anything else.</p>
  <p>The format and validate script runs <code>fmt -check</code> and, per module and environment, <code>init -backend=false</code> and <code>validate</code>, only if <code>terraform</code> or <code>tofu</code> is on the path; otherwise it prints that it skipped. A CI job installs Terraform with a pinned action, so those two run there. <code>plan</code> needs provider credentials and is not part of CI: run it per environment with that environment's credentials.</p>

  <h2>Destroy without orphans</h2>
  <p>After <code>terraform destroy</code> for an environment, save the listings and check them:</p>
  <pre><code>{`terraform -chdir=deploy/terraform/aws/environments/dev state list > state.txt
aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --tag-filters Key=scmle-environment,Values=dev > listing.json
python scripts/check_destroy_orphans.py --provider aws --environment dev --listing listing.json --state state.txt

gcloud asset search-all-resources --scope=projects/PROJECT --query='labels.scmle-environment=dev' --format=json > listing.json
python scripts/check_destroy_orphans.py --provider gcp --environment dev --listing listing.json --state state.txt`}</code></pre>
  <p>Exit 0 means nothing remains in the state or in the listing. Resources the provider removes later by design (a KMS key in its deletion window, a secret scheduled for deletion, a GCP resource in <code>DELETE_REQUESTED</code>) are listed as pending, not as orphans. Dev and staging set <code>destroyable = true</code> so buckets and registries do not block the destroy; production keeps it <code>false</code>. A workload identity pool deleted on GCP cannot reuse its ID for 30 days.</p>

  <h2>Not claimed</h2>
  <p>Applying these modules, a clean destroy on a real account, recorded cost, multi-region availability, and any cost or latency equivalence between the two providers. The next step, releasing a model onto this infrastructure, is on the <a href={`${base}/docs/cloud-release/`}>Cloud release</a> page.</p>

  <p class="meta">Infrastructure of the 0.1 beta line</p>
</DocsLayout>
